## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Expect My Future to Be... – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 37.5%;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Goals</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 37.5%;"><b>Theme:</b> I Expect My Future to Be...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow audio narratives. Interactive: Engage in role-play exercises to resolve conflicts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow audio narratives about educational goals and demonstrate understanding by paraphrasing key ideas and intentions (e.g., "What does Carlos want to achieve?" "He wants to get a degree in engineering").<br>Can engage in role-play exercises about future education, showing comprehension by following dialogue steps (e.g., "What will Ana study next?" "Science").<br>Can answer simple questions about peers' plans, demonstrating understanding of future education topics (e.g., "What is Carlos' goal?" "To be a teacher").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing three picture cards of different future professions (e.g., an engineer, a chef, a teacher). The teacher points to each picture and asks, "What is this job? What do you want to be in the <b>future</b>?" Students brainstorm ideas in pairs and share a few aloud. This activates prior knowledge about career <b>goals</b> and future aspirations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes the core vocabulary words on the board: <b>future, goals, plan, business, career, opportunity, vision, development</b>. The teacher pronounces each word clearly, modeling correct stress and intonation. For example, the teacher models "I have a <b>plan</b> for my <b>future</b>. My <b>goal</b> is to have a successful <b>career</b>." The teacher also models the pronunciation of /θ/ in "math" and /s/ in "mass" by clearly enunciating "I have to study <b>math</b> for my <b>career</b>, it's a huge <b>mass</b> of knowledge!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is a 'goal'? Is it something you want to do now or in the future?" (Expected answer: In the future/something you want to achieve.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I have a vision for my career,' what does 'vision' mean? Is it what I see right now or what I imagine for later?" (Expected answer: What you imagine for later/your future plan.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio narrative (approx. 1.5-2 minutes) featuring two 8th-grade students, Carlos and Ana, discussing their future educational and career <b>goals</b>. The audio will incorporate the target grammar (modals "have to," "must," WH-questions for goals, future continuous) and vocabulary (<b>future, goals, plan, business, career, opportunity, vision, development, ambitious, important, future-oriented, plan, achieve, prepare, set</b>). For instance, Carlos might say: "I <b>have to</b> study hard because I want to get a degree in engineering. In five years, I <b>will be working</b> as an engineer. What are your <b>plans</b>, Ana? What do you <b>want to</b> achieve?" Ana might respond: "I <b>must</b> finish high school first. I <b>plan to</b> study science. My <b>vision</b> is to work in environmental <b>development</b>."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets main ideas and specific details in short, simple audio texts about personal and academic aspirations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> The teacher asks students to listen once and answer: "Who is talking? What are they talking about?" (Expected answers: Two students, their future plans/goals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (Details):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with True/False statements or matching tasks. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: Carlos wants to be a doctor. (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Match: Ana's plan is to study _____. (Science)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific information regarding <b>future plans and goals</b>, identifying modals of necessity ("have to," "must"), WH-questions for educational/career goals, future continuous, and core vocabulary (<b>future, goals, plan, business, career, opportunity, vision, development, ambitious, important, future-oriented, plan, achieve, prepare, set</b>). Focus on pronunciation of future tense structures and modals (e.g., "I will study hard." "I have to be the best student in my class.") and phonemic awareness of minimal pairs /θ/ (math) and /s/ (mass).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice (Listening Cloze):</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students have a transcript with specific words or phrases omitted (e.g., modals, future continuous verbs, or key vocabulary words like <b>career, opportunity, development</b>). They listen and fill in the blanks. Example: "Carlos: I ______ to study hard because I want to get a degree in engineering. In five years, I ______ working as an engineer." (Expected answers: have, will be).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Listen and Select):</b> The teacher provides a list of WH-questions (e.g., "What does Carlos plan to study?", "When will Ana start her science studies?", "Why <b>must</b> Ana finish high school?") and multiple-choice answers for each. Students listen to the audio again and select the correct answer. This activity reinforces listening for details and question comprehension.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check:</b> Students compare their answers from the cloze and multiple-choice activities with a partner. They discuss any discrepancies and re-listen to specific parts of the audio if necessary to confirm their understanding. This encourages collaborative listening and peer feedback, allowing students to clarify their listening comprehension.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Role-play – "My Future Advice"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. The teacher assigns roles: Student A is a student with a future plan and a small conflict/challenge related to it (e.g., "I want to study <b>business</b>, but I think it's too expensive, so I don't know what to do. I <b>have to</b> make a <b>plan</b>."). Student B is a friend who listens carefully to Student A's problem and offers encouragement and suggestions based on what they heard, using target vocabulary, modals ("You <b>must</b> think about the <b>opportunity</b>," "You <b>have to</b> prepare <b>carefully</b>"), and WH-questions to gather more information (e.g., "What are your specific <b>goals</b>?", "<b>When</b> do you plan to start?"). The role-play requires active listening from Student B to provide relevant advice and demonstrate understanding of Student A's challenge. This task integrates discussing goals, responding to questions, and offering encouragement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe students' performance during the "My Future Advice" role-play, focusing on listening comprehension and appropriate responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "My Future Advice" role-play in front of the teacher or in small groups. The teacher particularly observes Student B's ability to listen to Student A's problem and respond with relevant questions and encouragement, paraphrasing key ideas from Student A's statements (e.g., "So, your goal is to study business, but the cost is the problem?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates understanding of peer's future plan/conflict through questions and advice (Listening Comprehension).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., <b>goal, plan, career, opportunity</b>) and 1-2 modals (e.g., <b>have to, must</b>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of future tense structures and modals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages collaboratively in the discussion, offering encouragement.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two quick sentences before leaving:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned ______ new words about the future."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I am confident in understanding audio about future plans because ______."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's lesson helped you most to understand future plans?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you still want to learn about discussing future goals?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson, especially regarding listening comprehension activities? (e.g., "The audio narrative was engaging and provided good context for the new vocabulary and grammar.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension)? (e.g., "I need to ensure the reading text for Lesson 2 prominently features the same vocabulary introduced today, reinforcing the connection between listening and reading comprehension.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will read a text about someone's future plans and goals, using the same vocabulary and grammatical structures introduced in today's listening lesson. This will help them transfer their understanding from auditory input to written text.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>